So this is about inference from an assumption, not the classical
implication, which is *defined* so that the statement ~p v q <-> p -> q is
true. In other words nibli does not exclusively involve truth values of
propositions, but rather also involves the ability to actually deduce
something from something else, whereas the classical implication does
(whatever the truth value of ~p v q is, the truth value of p -> q is the
same, because then whenever p is true q must be true). That was what I was
trying to get at.

And Luke: I was just constructing a nonsense proposition to be my
antecedent.

